# Keeps svc-<app> units off Shelley's port. The reasons are here; the units
# and docs that mention the guard point to this file.
#
# Shelley listens on 127.0.0.1:9999 and trusts any request that carries an
# X-Exedev-Userid header, which exe.dev's proxy sets and any local process can
# forge. Shelley runs as exedev, who has passwordless sudo, so a compromised
# service that could reach the port would be root. The hardening profile cannot
# close this itself: IPAddressDeny= has no notion of ports.
#
# 61184-65519 is the range systemd allocates DynamicUser= UIDs from, the users
# every svc-<app> unit runs as. It is fixed when systemd is built, and CI
# checks it against the image's systemd. A unit that sets User= to a static
# user is outside it, one more reason docs/deploying.md forbids that.
#
# exe.dev supplies the kernel and the image carries no modules, so the rule
# can use only what that kernel has built in. The ip family is enough: Shelley
# binds IPv4 only, and an AF_INET6 socket reaches 127.0.0.1 as IPv4. reject is
# a separate kernel feature from the nf_tables core; exe.dev's kernel has it
# (checked on a VM, 2026-11-09), and a refused connection is easier to
# diagnose than the timeout drop would give. Should a kernel lack it, the rule
# does not load and Shelley does not listen (shelley-guard-socket.conf).

# Replace the table if it is already loaded, so a restart of the unit works.
table ip shelley_guard
delete table ip shelley_guard

table ip shelley_guard {
	chain output {
		type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
		ip daddr 127.0.0.0/8 tcp dport 9999 meta skuid 61184-65519 reject with tcp reset
	}
}
